<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nova Scotia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nova Scotia is a province on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, originally part of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> acquired it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> in 1713, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">asiento</a>. These gains marked an important stage in the growth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nova-scotia.jpg" alt="Nova Scotia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Kooma, FJDEACKB, Zscout370 and Denelson83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nova_Scotia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Utrecht (1713) delivered the first great imperial windfall — Gibraltar, Minorca, <b>Nova Scotia</b>, Newfoundland, the Hudson Bay territories and the asiento (the contract to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America) — establishing Britain as a leading colonial power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The British Parliament passed the British North America Act, and on 1 July 1867 the Dominion of Canada came into being, uniting <b>Nova Scotia</b>, New Brunswick and the Province of Canada, which was divided into Ontario and Quebec.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Spanish Succession John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough, won at Ramillies in 1706, Oudenarde in 1708 and Malplaquet in 1709, and the Treaty of Utrecht (1713) gave Britain Gibraltar, Minorca, Newfoundland and <b>Nova Scotia</b> and the asiento to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nova Scotia</b> and the Province of Canada won responsible government, a ministry answerable to the elected assembly, in 1848; the Australian colonies and New Zealand followed in the 1850s, and the Dominion of Canada from 1867, the Commonwealth of Australia from 1901, the Union of South Africa and…</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…folklorist James Madison Carpenter recorded it in England, Scotland and Wales in the early 1930s, recordings kept by the Vaughan Williams Memorial Library; Helen Hartness Flanders recorded versions by old sailors in New England in the 1940s, and Helen Creighton in <b>Nova Scotia</b> in the 1940s and 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/songs/blow-the-man-down">Blow the Man Down</a></li><li><blockquote>…&quot;No More Auction Block&quot;, which, according to Alan Lomax, was sung by formerly enslaved people who fled to <b>Nova Scotia</b> after Britain abolished slavery in 1833; in 1978 Dylan told the journalist Marc Rowland that he had taken it from that spiritual and that his song followed the…</blockquote><a href="/songs/blowin-in-the-wind">Blowin' in the Wind</a></li></ul></section>
